WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.797
Even a fixed population produces different finite samples, so a statistic computed from those samples has

00:00:06.797 --> 00:00:13.393
its own distribution. Standard error measures the spread of an estimator, such as a sample mean,

00:00:13.393 --> 00:00:14.630
across repeated sampling.

00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:23.933
This example assumes independent normal observations with known standard deviation two. Repeated correlated video frames would

00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:25.033
violate independence.

00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:32.140
Averaging n independent measurements divides the variance by n, rather than dividing the standard deviation by

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:32.553
n.

00:00:33.067 --> 00:00:38.919
How many times more observations are needed to halve standard error? Pause here and make a

00:00:38.919 --> 00:00:40.017
prediction before continuing.

00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:49.961
Standard error measures sampling spread of the estimator. It is different from the spread of individual

00:00:49.961 --> 00:00:57.133
observations. For our starting inputs, Observations: 25. Standard error: 0.4. Interval half-width: 0.784.

00:00:57.633 --> 00:01:05.833
Under these stated assumptions, the repeated-sampling procedure has about 95% coverage. A realized interval is not

00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:09.933
a 95% probability statement about a fixed parameter.

00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:17.945
Every uncertainty calculation needs a sampling model. Bootstrap units and confidence-interval assumptions must match how the

00:01:17.945 --> 00:01:19.353
data was collected.

